Q: Given a whole hour of the day, how to find the next whole hour? A: <h2>The bulletproof solution</h2>
<p>This answer combines the best ideas from the answers by Louis Wasserman and by Rowan Horváth and provides the code.</p>
<p>Rowan Horváth is right that the bulletproof solution to iterating over the whole hours in your interval is to find all transitions of UTC offset within that interval. Then find all whole hours before the first transition, between each pair of consecutive transitions and after the last transition. So that is what I am doing.</p>
<h2>Code</h2>
<p>The code follows Louis Wasserman’s recommendation to avoid <code>ZonedDateTime</code>. It’s longer than the code you get from each of those two answers, but in return takes all possible combinations of transitions (gaps and overlaps) into account.</p>
<pre><code>/**
 * @return The instants of all whole hours in zoneId
 *         between start inclusive and end exclusive in chronological order.
 */
public static List&lt;Instant&gt; wholeHoursBetween(ZoneId zoneId,
                                              Instant start,
                                              Instant end) {
    // First find all time zone transitions between start and end, both exclusive.
    List&lt;ZoneOffsetTransition&gt; transitions = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    ZoneRules rules = zoneId.getRules();
    ZoneOffsetTransition transition = rules. nextTransition(start);
    while (transition != null &amp;&amp; transition.getInstant().isBefore(end)) {
        transitions.add(transition);
        transition = rules. nextTransition(transition.getInstant());
    }

    List&lt;Instant&gt; wholeHours = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    // For each part interval before, between and after the transitions,
    // find all whole hours using the UTC offset
    // in effect for that part interval.
    if (transitions.isEmpty()) {
        addWholeHours(wholeHours, start.atZone(zoneId).getOffset(),
                      start, end);
    } else {
        addWholeHours(wholeHours, start.atZone(zoneId).getOffset(),
                      start, transitions.getFirst().getInstant());
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; transitions.size() - 2; i++) {
            addWholeHours(wholeHours, transitions.get(i).getOffsetAfter(),
                          transitions.get(i).getInstant(), transitions.get(i + 1).getInstant());
        }
        addWholeHours(wholeHours, transitions.getLast().getOffsetAfter(),
                      transitions.getLast().getInstant(), end);
    }

    return wholeHours;
}

/**
 * Adds to wholeHours the instants of the whole hours at offset
 * between start inclusive and end exclusive
 */
private static void addWholeHours(List&lt;Instant&gt; wholeHours,
                                  ZoneOffset offset,
                                  Instant start,
                                  Instant end) {
    // Round start forward/up to the net whole hour
    Instant wholeHour = start.atOffset(offset)
            .plusHours(1)
            .minusNanos(1)
            .truncatedTo(ChronoUnit.HOURS)
            .toInstant();
    while (wholeHour.isBefore(end)) {
        wholeHours.add(wholeHour);
        wholeHour = wholeHour.plus(1, ChronoUnit.HOURS);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Trying it out</h2>
<p>Your example from Lord Howe island, taking an interval from 00:00 to 04:00:</p>
<pre><code>    var zoneId = ZoneId.of(&quot;Australia/Lord_Howe&quot;);
    var date = LocalDate.of(2026, Month.APRIL, 5);
    Instant start = date.atStartOfDay(zoneId).toInstant();
    Instant end = date.atTime(4, 0).atZone(zoneId).toInstant();
    List&lt;Instant&gt; wholeHours = wholeHoursBetween(zoneId, start, end);

    System.out.println(start + &quot; start&quot;);
    wholeHours.forEach(i
            -&gt; System.out.println(i + &quot; &quot; + i.atZone(zoneId).toLocalTime()));
    System.out.println(end + &quot; end&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Output shows the instants left and the local clock time (the whole hours) right:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>2026-04-04T13:00:00Z start
2026-04-04T13:00:00Z 00:00
2026-04-04T14:00:00Z 01:00
2026-04-04T15:30:00Z 02:00
2026-04-04T16:30:00Z 03:00
2026-04-04T17:30:00Z end
</code></pre>
<p>(The demonstration code does use <code>ZonedDateTime</code> as an intermediate step for converting from <code>Instant</code> to <code>LocalTime</code>, which is Java’s way of making that conversion.)</p>
<p>The opposite transition is a bit more interesting. When Summer time begins in October, the clocks are turned forward from 02:00 to 02:30, so the time 02:00 does not exist and should not be in the output.</p>
<pre><code>    var date = LocalDate.of(2025, Month.OCTOBER, 5);
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>2025-10-04T13:30:00Z start
2025-10-04T13:30:00Z 00:00
2025-10-04T14:30:00Z 01:00
2025-10-04T16:00:00Z 03:00
2025-10-04T17:00:00Z end
</code></pre>
<p>As expected 02:00 is not there. The instants to the left tells that there are one and a half hours between 01:00 and 03:00.</p>

Q: Audio tag GUI not visible A: <blockquote>
<p>The  element has no intrinsic visual output of its own unless the controls attribute is specified, in which case the browser's default controls are shown.<br />
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/audio#styling_with_css</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Note:</p>
<h4>DevTools in Chrome will show:</h4>
<pre><code>(user agent stylesheet)
audio:not([controls]) {
    display: none !important;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If a CSS is created in an attempt to override it:</p>
<pre><code>audio:not([controls]) {
    display: inline !important;
}
</code></pre>
<p>DevTools will cross out the (user agent stylesheet), leaving <code>display: inline !important;</code> as &quot;active&quot;.<br />
<strong>But it's not.</strong> As could be seen in <em>Computed</em> tab.</p>
<h4>DevTools in Firefox</h4>
<p>Same as Chrome, but it's not showing browser stylesheets by default (Option &gt;
Show browser styles).<br />
When it's not shown, even low specificity</p>
<pre><code>audio, canvas, video {
  display: inline-block;
}
</code></pre>
<p>will look like it's the active property. <strong>But it's not.</strong></p>
